      "content": "No, historical reliance on specific skin tones, facial features, or ancestral heritages to signal \"old money\" is a widely criticized and outdated marketing practice that ultimately fails to capture the modern reality of global luxury consumers. Cities like Paris, London, and New York are home to wealthy individuals of diverse backgrounds who do not fit a single racial or ethnic mold, and targeting a narrow visual profile often excludes the very millennials and Gen Z fighters who drive current trends. If your goal is true authenticity in your ad creative, you should focus these visual assets on narratives about heritage, craftsmanship, and timeless style rather than relying on rigid aesthetic cues that may unintentionally alienate valid segments of your ultimate \"old money\" audience.",
